Description: Dim unfocused windows and draw window drop shadows
 In the X11 session picom dims unfocused windows and draws shadows under
 them. The sway session had neither: the window with keyboard focus
 looked like every other window, a floating window had no edge against
 what lay underneath, and the SwayFX configuration lines for these
 effects made sway -C fail as unknown commands.
 .
 New settings, with SwayFX's names, arguments and defaults:
   default_dim_inactive <0.0-1.0>               (0.0)
   dim_inactive <0.0-1.0>                       per window: criteria, for_window
   dim_inactive_colors.unfocused <#RRGGBB[AA]>  (#000000FF)
   dim_inactive_colors.urgent <#RRGGBB[AA]>     (#900000FF)
   shadows enable|disable                       (disable)
   shadows_on_csd enable|disable                (disable)
   shadow_blur_radius <0-99>                    (20)
   shadow_color <#RRGGBB[AA]>                   (#0000007F)
   shadow_inactive_color <#RRGGBB[AA]>          (#0000007F)
   shadow_offset <x> <y>                        (0 0)
 .
 SwayFX draws these in its own GLES2 renderer (scenefx). Here both are
 plain wlroots scene buffers holding small CPU-rendered images, so they
 work with every renderer, pixman included, and none of them takes
 pointer input. A window is dimmed when its border would not be drawn in
 the focused colours: a uniform overlay covers its surface and border,
 not its title bar. A shadow is nine pieces of one small template with
 coverage alpha * (1 - d/r)^2 at distance d from the window's box (the
 shadow colour's alpha inside the box when r is 0). Tiled windows cast
 it into a per-workspace layer just below the tiling layer, so it shows
 in the gaps and never on a neighbour; floating windows cast it at the
 bottom of their own tree, over tiled and lower floating windows. A
 shadow is clipped to its output; fullscreen, hidden and scratchpad
 windows cast none. With the defaults nothing changes on screen.
 .
 Also: a reload commits the transaction it arranges instead of waiting
 for the next event; clearing urgency refreshes the window's decorations
 at once, as setting it does; and for_window checks the argument of
 dim_inactive when the rule is read, so sway -C fails on a rule that
 could never apply.
 .
 Not forwarded: sway declines compositing effects
 (https://github.com/swaywm/sway/issues/3380,
 https://github.com/swaywm/sway/pull/4254).
